want to watch this tutorial in its entirety all 5 hours of it then go ahead I did put it together in a way that makes sense if you watch it in order and so all of the nodes that I use tend to build on Knowledge from previous nodes so I hope that you like this video if you keep coming back to it and you find it useful please go ahead leave a like leave a comment you can do crazy crazy cool things with Copernicus so I really encourage you to mess around with it even this basic little intro that I made was maybe a 30 minute setup that I did and it's mostly cops I did\n\n**[00:01:14]** Foam grain I did fo breath gate weave halation I use the clad kny patterns to make the formations just there's so much that you can do with cernus and cops so I do hope that you find Value in this video let's not waste any time and just\n\n\n\n<a id=\"chap-02\"></a>\n### 02. CORE CONCEPTS [00:01:29]\n\n> [Lien direct YouTube vers ce chapitre (00:01:29)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPL215vfNwg&t=89s)\n\n**[00:01:29]** Get straight into it now I've tried to cover as many of these nodes as I possibly can if I've left anything out or if I've misunderstood a node please be sure to leave a comment so that I can get around to fixing it I'll release an updated version of this tutorial when the new version of Houdini is released as of Houdini 20.5 370 this is all up to date so it should work perfectly fine if you're using anything up to that version so before we get into all of these nodes I'm just going to go ahead and explain some Core Concepts for working with Copernicus or cups as I'm going to be referring to it so cups is just the new\n\n**[00:02:05]** Compositing context released in 20.5 and you can create a cop Network at pretty much any level I believe so if we create a cop network over here at the object level you'll see it just creates this it has some parameters and you can dive inside and it takes you into the Copernicus context now if you are working at the geometry level that's the only time that the node is going to appear different if we drop a cup Network here you'll see that it actually acts as a S node it's got a few other options as well as an output right so you can use an external cop and that would be if you want to reference one that isn't at that level so say you want to reference this one out here you can do that you\n\n**[00:02:40]** Can reference that one at the object level however if you aren't going to reference anything you can double click on this and dive inside this will also take you to the kernus context over here we can drop something like a constant don't worry about the node too much this will all be explained as we go now once you place that node you will see an output appearing in two different places firstly in your viewport you'll have this white square and in your composite view you'll also have this white square in the composite view you're viewing this as an image in the scene view you're viewing this as a volume but it's actually the same thing so this is something that you don't really need to worry about too much but the idea is\n\n**[00:03:16]** This is a pixel representation of a voxal grid and we can actually see that this is a voxal grid if we go back up to our s level and middle Mass on our cop Network you'll see that we have one grid volume it doesn't have a name but it is 1, 24x24 voxels and so it's a 2d grid\n\n**[00:03:35]** Where each pixel is represented by a single voxel the resolution of the grid is defined by the Default Resolution over here otherwise it can be specified inside of the c network as well if you use something like a size reference which we'll get into later but as you can see if we increase this to something like 4096 by 496 you'll see that we now have a much higher resolution so there's more voils making up this grid it does still stay the same size but the voxal size is reduced so we can just set this back to the default and keep in mind that this can be treated like a regular volume so you can give this a name\n\n**[00:04:10]** Something like this we you do a name and you can call it something like density and you can actually use it for things like an attribute from map where you bring it into second input and you can fetch data like that alternatively you can also do things like a volume sample okay so let's say that we're inside of this node what can we do and how how does it work so this is going to be a horizontal workflow so you're going to be working from left to right where your inputs are on the left your outputs on the right so let's just Tab and type fractal noise so fractal noise and fractal noise 3D if we drop that we have\n\n**[00:04:45]** A noise pattern applied to that grid that we saw earlier once again this is going to be a 1024x 1024 volume it now\n\n**[00:04:54]** Just has some data applied to it so each voxel is treated as a pixel and each pixel has a value we can and play around with things like the amplitude of our noise and make adjustments to this now if you want to reference something that's inside of a cop network but you don't necessarily want to use it as an output there is a way to do that and I'll just show this by dropping something like a rubber toy and then using a UV quick shade in this UV quick shade if we want to reference that noise that we just created we can do something like a null and if we plug this into the null we can give this a name like texture out then all we have to do is copy this node's path so we can select\n\n**[00:05:30]** And pressl C go uper level and on this quick shade under the texture map press crl V and it'll paste the path to that node right if we click away it won't actually work and that's because there's one special piece of syntax that we need and that is just the op prefix so it's up colon the path right so op colon path\n\n**[00:05:50]** And then that will now fetch this particular texture and apply it as a texture map and you can do this in any context so if you're creating materials you can actually do the exact same thing and do this for things like your diffuse color or roughness or displacement whatever you need you can just do this op colon syntax with the location so with the path to the node that you want to reference now inside of this c network let's take a look at a few other things as you can see on this noise being output here there is a blue output now blue is for a mono and you will also see on the note that this is a mono this\n\n**[00:06:27]** Just means that it is a single component value the same as float so it can be any decimal value positive or negative and so each one of these pixels can have any negative decimal value or any positive decimal value but it is a single component if we switch this to UV that makes it a 2d noise so what we now have is a red Channel and a green Channel and you'll see that this changes to an orange output that just means that it's a UV if we change this to RGB this gives us three components so now we're looking at red green and blue and this changes our output to this green type then we\n\n**[00:07:02]** Have rgba and this is just red green blue with an alpha Channel and this gets shown as a yellow output the other types that you might see is something like this which is a geometry output and so if I just dive inside of here and drop something like a grid what this gives us is an actual piece of geometry and there are certain nodes at the cop level that will allow you to work with geometry but most of the time you're going to be working with mon UV RGB rgba the only other one that you should be aware of is something like ID and ID is just a unique integer value per ID tile now in\n\n**[00:07:41]** This situation where we have the tile pattern node which we will get into later or you can skip ahead if you want to find out more about it this generates a bunch of tiles and each tile has a unique ID and now you will notice that we actually have more than one output on this node so on this tile pattern node in the bottom right we have more than one output you can see that we have a mono we have an ID and we have UVS if you want to change what you're previewing you can switch between these so click on the bottom right corner of this node where it says tiles and you can switch to ID or UV now do keep in mind that this is just for preview purposes if we go uper level what is being output is whatever you have your\n\n**[00:08:18]** Display flag on so it'll be the first output of whatever you have your display flag on at this level we're just previewing and you can actually see that it says viewing output 2 we can switch this back to just the tiles and then that will just disappear so as you can see when we switch it it tells us which output we are viewing do keep in mind that a lot of the time you can't just switch between signatures so having a signature of mono you won't just be able to go to an RGB input you can see that inputs have a signature and outputs have a signature so something like a fractal noise 3D is expecting an RGB value for an input and then it can output whatever\n\n**[00:08:54]** Signature we choose over here so when we choose signature this is for the output so sometimes you may need to do conversions and there are a bunch of conversion Options under the layer tab you can see we have all sorts of conversions and we'll be going through all of that later in this video now the last thing before we actually get into this video is I just wanted to mention some of the things that are hidden from us because often times in Houdini the most complicated things are the things that are hidden so when we drop something like a constant or even a noise this will always have a range right so any of these values will have a\n\n**[00:09:31]** Particular range if you were to drop a constant and let's just give it a value of one you can see that one appears as white and zero appears as black but if you go to something like five that also appears as white so does 10 and the thing is any negative number also appears as black and this is important because we need to know the actual values that we're working with and we can't just assume that black is going to be zero and one is going to be white now if we have the situation where we have - 10 as the value how would we know about that now one way that we can test with this is if we go over to the composite\n\n**[00:10:06]** View and in the composite view we can right click and say inspect and if we take a look at this you can see that it has in the bottom left of our inspect panel a value of minus 10 if I push this up to a value of 10 you can see it now shows a value of 10 and if you were to do something like a clamp so now we're taking this and we're clamping it to a maximum of one you can see now that the maximum value is one even though there is no visible difference between these two right do keep in mind that your values can extend beyond one and appear as white or go below zero and appear as black the only other thing I want to mention is the other data that gets\n\n**[00:10:42]** Passed through with each and every node if we take a look at this information on the actual cup Network you'll see that we have resolution pixel scale default border default precision and tile visualization and all of those settings are what are known as layer properties now when we drop something like a constant node the reason that it is this resolution is because it's fetching the layer property from up here and this is what we call a layer and so this layer has a particular resolution now if you were to create something like a size reference then the size reference can have its own settings for example activate the resolution and this layer's\n\n**[00:11:19]** Layer properties are different to this one right so they are layer properties that get passed along with a particular node that you may not necessarily see but these are those values right so all of these on here these are just layer properties and the last thing that I just wanted to mention is the space that we're going to be working in with cops if we look in our viewport you can see that our range doesn't go from0 to one\n\n**[00:11:47]** Right commonly if you're working with something like UVS the space is going to be 0 to one and so it would start 0 0 and move to the top right and become 1 one but when we're working in cernus it's actually minus one minus one has the bottom left and one one in the top right where we have zero right in the middle and that's just important to know because sometimes we may be working with a position map or UVS and we're expecting one space but getting another so cernus is going to be in this minus1 -1 to one one space and I think that's all we really need as a foundation to get into this tutorial so let's get started with our first note so the first\n\n\n\n<a id=\"chap-03\"></a>\n### 03. CONSTANT [00:12:22]\n\n"},"started_at_ms":1789215344591,"completed_at_ms":1789215344591}}
