network over here at the object level you'll see it just creates this it has some parameters and you can dive inside and it takes you into the Copernicus context now if you are working at the geometry level that's the only time that the node is going to appear different if we drop a cup Network here you'll see that it actually acts as a S node it's got a few other options as well as an output right so you can use an external cop and that would be if you want to reference one that isn't at that level so say you want to reference this one out here you can do that you\\n\\n**[00:02:40]** Can reference that one at the object level however if you aren't going to reference anything you can double click on this and dive inside this will also take you to the kernus context over here we can drop something like a constant don't worry about the node too much this will all be explained as we go now once you place that node you will see an output appearing in two different places firstly in your viewport you'll have this white square and in your composite view you'll also have this white square in the composite view you're viewing this as an image in the scene view you're viewing this as a volume but it's actually the same thing so this is something that you don't really need to worry about too much but the idea is\\n\\n**[00:03:16]** This is a pixel representation of a voxal grid and we can actually see that this is a voxal grid if we go back up to our s level and middle Mass on our cop Network you'll see that we have one grid volume it doesn't have a name but it is 1, 24x24 voxels and so it's a 2d grid\\n\\n**[00:03:35]** Where each pixel is represented by a single voxel the resolution of the grid is defined by the Default Resolution over here otherwise it can be specified inside of the c network as well if you use something like a size reference which we'll get into later but as you can see if we increase this to something like 4096 by 496 you'll see that we now have a much h